  "prediction": "The report provides an extensive list of resources for understanding legislative procedure in the United States Congress. The resources include books, videos, glossaries, and training programs. Some of these materials are produced by the Government Publishing Office (GPO) and are available to the public, while others are only accessible to congressional offices.\n\nThe report begins by listing several books on legislative procedure, including \"Congressional Deskbook,\" \"Congressional Procedures and the Policy Process,\" and \"Congressional Quarterly's American Congressional Dictionary.\" These resources cover various aspects of the legislative process, from the practical to the comprehensive.\n\nThe report also mentions several videos and training programs offered by the Congressional Research Service (CRS). For example, CRS offers a video titled \"An Act of Congress\" and a series of nine brief video clips explaining the legislative process on Congress.gov. CRS also provides training programs for congressional staff, including the Budget Process Institutes and the monthly introductory class, Legislative Concepts.\n\nAdditionally, the report lists several online glossaries, including the Congressional Quarterly's Legislative Glossary and the United States Senate Glossary. These resources provide definitions and explanations of key terms related to legislative procedure.\n\nFinally, the report mentions that some of the works on legislative procedure are only available to congressional offices, such as those produced by the House and Senate Parliamentarians. These resources are essential for understanding the rules and procedures of the House and Senate.\n\nOverall, the report provides a comprehensive list of resources for anyone interested in learning about legislative procedure in the United States Congress. Whether you are a student, a researcher, or a congressional staffer, these resources will help you gain a deeper understanding of the legislative process.",
